  "query_preview": "..\nMonday afternoon through Tuesday: Cumulus clouds and scattered \nshower activity will develop Monday afternoon over the higher\nterrain along the Canadian border as surface heating steepens \nlapse rates. CAPE values look rather pitiful (50-70 J/kg) so\nlightning is not expected with these showers. High temperatures\nwill remain around 10 degrees below average in the mid to upper\n60s. Heading into Tuesday, a mid-level wave drops down into the\nregion from the north. With this wave will come additional \nchances for showers - this time over a broader area including \nthe Cascades, northern WA mountains, and northern ID Panhandle.\nPrecip amounts are looking light both Monday and Tuesday with \ntotal rainfall accumulations by Tuesday afternoon generally \nranging from a few hundredths to a tenth of an inch. The North \nCascades and areas of higher terrain along the Canadian border \nhave a 25 to 45 percent chance of receiving a quarter inch or\nmore of precip.\n\nWednesday through Sunday: By Wednesday the mid-level wave drops\nsouth of the region over southern Oregon and northern \nCalifornia, while higher heights nudge in over Eastern WA and\nNorth ID, prompting a warming and drying trend. The dry weather\nwill be briefly interrupted Thursday evening into Friday as the\nmid- level wave to the south shifts northeastward toward Montana,\nbringing a 20 to 40 percent chance of showers and thunderstorms\nover the southeastern corner of WA and the Central Panhandle \nmountains. By Saturday, the wave will have exited to the east,\nand ridging will rebound across the region favoring continued\nwarm and dry conditions. Afternoon highs Wednesday through\nSunday will range from the mid 70s to low 80s, and overnight\nlows will fall into the 40s and 50s. Overall, weather for the\nweek will be typical of mid-September with minimal impacts\nexpected. /Fewkes",
